{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KXesNcRys2hvtsmCBAHzGY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd018-head\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/head: Print the First Lines or Bytes of Files\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Scripts and interactive users use head to preview the beginning of files or to extract\n6\t  a fixed number of lines from pipeline output. The macOS BSD head differs from GNU head\n7\t  in byte-mode support, negative counts, and multi-file header formatting. The Go\n8\t  implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (ghead, installed by\n9\t  coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations and multi-file output, verified by\n10\t  differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n11\t\n12\tgoals:\n13\t  - G1: Print the first N lines of each input file, defaulting to 10 lines.\n14\t  - G2: Support byte-count mode (-c) as an alternative to line-count mode (-n).\n15\t  - G3: Support negative counts that print all content except the last N lines or bytes.\n16\t  - G4: Print filename headers when processing multiple files, with -q and -v to control header display.\n17\t\n18\trequirements:\n19\t  R1:\n20\t    title: Line-count mode (default and -n)\n21\t    items:\n22\t      - R1.1:\n23\t          text: When no flags are given, must print the first 10 lines of each input file.\n24\t      - R1.2:\n25\t          text: \"-n NUM or --lines=NUM must print the first NUM lines. NUM must be a positive integer or a negative integer prefixed with '-'.\"\n26\t      - R1.3:\n27\t          text: \"When NUM is prefixed with '-' (e.g., -n -5), must print all lines except the last NUM lines. This requires buffering the entire input to determine where to stop.\"\n28\t      - R1.4:\n29\t          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given or when a file argument is \"-\".\n30\t      - R1.5:\n31\t          text: A line is terminated by a newline character. The last line of a file that does not end with a newline is still counted as a line.\n32\t\n33\t  R2:\n34\t    title: Byte-count mode (-c)\n35\t    items:\n36\t      - R2.1:\n37\t          text: \"-c NUM or --bytes=NUM must print the first NUM bytes of each input. -c and -n are mutually exclusive; the last one given takes precedence.\"\n38\t      - R2.2:\n39\t          text: \"When NUM is prefixed with '-' (e.g., -c -100), must print all bytes except the last NUM bytes.\"\n40\t      - R2.3:\n41\t          text: NUM may use multiplier suffixes as defined by GNU coreutils. At minimum, must support b (512), K or KiB (1024), M or MiB (1048576), and G or GiB (1073741824).\n42\t\n43\t  R3:\n44\t    title: Multi-file output and headers\n45\t    items:\n46\t      - R3.1:\n47\t          text: \"When multiple files are given, must precede each file's output with a header in the format '==> FILENAME <==' followed by a newline. A blank line separates each file's header from the previous file's output.\"\n48\t      - R3.2:\n49\t          text: When a single file is given, must not print a header by default.\n50\t      - R3.3:\n51\t          text: -q or --quiet or --silent must suppress all headers, even for multiple files.\n52\t      - R3.4:\n53\t          text: -v or --verbose must print headers even for a single file.\n54\t      - R3.5:\n55\t          text: When a named file cannot be opened, must print an error message to stderr and continue processing remaining files.\n56\t\n57\t  R4:\n58\t    title: Exit codes and differential testing\n59\t    items:\n60\t      - R4.1:\n61\t          text: Must exit 0 when all input files are processed successfully.\n62\t      - R4.2:\n63\t          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read. Must still process and output content for the files that were successfully read.\n64\t      - R4.3:\n65\t          text: Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\n66\t      - R4.4:\n67\t          text: \"Tests must cover: default 10 lines, explicit -n count, -c byte count, negative counts (-n -5, -c -100), multi-file headers, -q (suppress headers), -v (force headers), stdin input, and error on non-existent file.\"\n68\t\n69\tnon_goals:\n70\t  - cmd/head does not implement -z (zero-terminated lines using NUL delimiter). This flag adds complexity to the line-counting logic and is rarely used.\n71\t  - cmd/head does not implement the full range of GNU multiplier suffixes (kB, MB, GB decimal variants). Only binary suffixes (K/KiB, M/MiB, G/GiB, b) are supported.\n72\t  - cmd/head does not follow files or re-read on change; it reads each input once.\n73\t\n74\tpackage_contract:\n75\t  imports:\n76\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sizeparse\n77\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n78\t\n79\tacceptance_criteria:\n80\t  - id: AC1\n81\t    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/head produces lines 1 through 10, matching ghead.\"\n82\t    traces:\n83\t      - R1.1\n84\t      - R1.2\n85\t      - R1.3\n86\t      - R1.4\n87\t      - R1.5\n88\t  - id: AC2\n89\t    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/head -n 5 produces lines 1 through 5, matching ghead.\"\n90\t    traces:\n91\t      - R1.2\n92\t      - R1.3\n93\t      - R2.1\n94\t      - R4.4\n95\t  - id: AC3\n96\t    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/head -n -5 produces lines 1 through 15 (all but last 5), matching ghead.\"\n97\t    traces:\n98\t      - R1.2\n99\t      - R1.3\n100\t      - R2.1\n101\t      - R4.4\n102\t  - id: AC4\n103\t    criterion: \"printf 'abcdefghij' | go run ./cmd/head -c 5 produces 'abcde', matching ghead.\"\n104\t    traces:\n105\t      - R2.1\n106\t      - R2.2\n107\t      - R4.4\n108\t  - id: AC5\n109\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/head file1.txt file2.txt prints headers '==> file1.txt <==' and '==> file2.txt <==' with correct content, matching ghead.\"\n110\t    traces:\n111\t      - R1.1\n112\t      - R1.2\n113\t      - R1.3\n114\t      - R1.4\n115\t      - R1.5\n116\t  - id: AC6\n117\t    criterion: \"Differential test against ghead passes for default, -n, -c, negative counts, multi-file, -q, -v, stdin, and error cases.\"\n118\t    traces:\n119\t      - R1.2\n120\t      - R1.3\n121\t      - R1.4\n122\t      - R2.1\n123\t      - R2.2\n124\t      - R2.3\n125\t      - R3.1\n126\t      - R3.3\n127\t      - R3.4\n128\t      - R3.5\n129\t      - R4.3\n130\t      - R4.4\n131\t  - id: AC7\n132\t    criterion: \"cmd/head compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n133\t    traces:\n134\t      - R1.1\n135\t      - R1.2\n136\t      - R1.3\n137\t      - R1.4\n138\t      - R1.5\n139\t      - R2.1\n140\t      - R2.2\n141\t      - R2.3\n142\t      - R3.1\n143\t      - R3.2\n144\t      - R3.3\n145\t      - R3.4\n146\t      - R3.5\n147\t      - R4.1\n148\t      - R4.2\n149\t      - R4.3\n150\t      - R4.4\n151\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a31301b-1723-4b6e-95a8-54479b7356ac","uuid":"c1eabbcc-e741-482c-94f8-6a02060abeb9","timestamp":"2026-05-14T12:42:23.129Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5162/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd018-head.yaml","content":"id: srd018-head\ntitle: \"cmd/head: Print the First Lines or Bytes of Files\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Scripts and interactive users use head to preview the beginning of files or to extract\n  a fixed number of lines from pipeline output. The macOS BSD head differs from GNU head\n  in byte-mode support, negative counts, and multi-file header formatting. The Go\n  implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (ghead, installed by\n  coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations and multi-file output, verified by\n  differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Print the first N lines of each input file, defaulting to 10 lines.\n  - G2: Support byte-count mode (-c) as an alternative to line-count mode (-n).\n  - G3: Support negative counts that print all content except the last N lines or bytes.\n  - G4: Print filename headers when processing multiple files, with -q and -v to control header display.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Line-count mode (default and -n)\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: When no flags are given, must print the first 10 lines of each input file.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: \"-n NUM or --lines=NUM must print the first NUM lines. NUM must be a positive integer or a negative integer prefixed with '-'.\"\n      - R1.3:\n          text: \"When NUM is prefixed with '-' (e.g., -n -5), must print all lines except the last NUM lines. This requires buffering the entire input to determine where to stop.\"\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given or when a file argument is \"-\".\n      - R1.5:\n          text: A line is terminated by a newline character. The last line of a file that does not end with a newline is still counted as a line.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Byte-count mode (-c)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: \"-c NUM or --bytes=NUM must print the first NUM bytes of each input. -c and -n are mutually exclusive; the last one given takes precedence.\"\n      - R2.2:\n          text: \"When NUM is prefixed with '-' (e.g., -c -100), must print all bytes except the last NUM bytes.\"\n      - R2.3:\n          text: NUM may use multiplier suffixes as defined by GNU coreutils. At minimum, must support b (512), K or KiB (1024), M or MiB (1048576), and G or GiB (1073741824).\n\n  R3:\n    title: Multi-file output and headers\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: \"When multiple files are given, must precede each file's output with a header in the format '==> FILENAME <==' followed by a newline. A blank line separates each file's header from the previous file's output.\"\n      - R3.2:\n          text: When a single file is given, must not print a header by default.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: -q or --quiet or --silent must suppress all headers, even for multiple files.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: -v or --verbose must print headers even for a single file.\n      - R3.5:\n          text: When a named file cannot be opened, must print an error message to stderr and continue processing remaining files.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all input files are processed successfully.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read. Must still process and output content for the files that were successfully read.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: Differential tests must compare stdout output and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\n      - R4.4:\n          text: \"Tests must cover: default 10 lines, explicit -n count, -c byte count, negative counts (-n -5, -c -100), multi-file headers, -q (suppress headers), -v (force headers), stdin input, and error on non-existent file.\"\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/head does not implement -z (zero-terminated lines using NUL delimiter). This flag adds complexity to the line-counting logic and is rarely used.\n  - cmd/head does not implement the full range of GNU multiplier suffixes (kB, MB, GB decimal variants). Only binary suffixes (K/KiB, M/MiB, G/GiB, b) are supported.\n  - cmd/head does not follow files or re-read on change; it reads each input once.\n\npackage_contract:\n  imports:\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sizeparse\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/head produces lines 1 through 10, matching ghead.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R1.5\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"seq 1 20 | go run ./cmd/head -n 5 produces lines 1 through 5, matching ghead.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R2.1\n      - R4.4\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \