{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0119pDN4P6HFYcpYj9bkdAs8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n2\t// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n3\t\n4\tpackage sys\n5\t\n6\timport (\n7\t\t\"os\"\n8\t\t\"os/signal\"\n9\t\t\"syscall\"\n10\t)\n11\t\n12\t// InstallSIGPIPEHandler installs a SIGPIPE handler that causes the process to\n13\t// exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer, matching\n14\t// GNU coreutils behavior.\n15\t// R1.5: uses signal.Notify with a buffered channel; does not use signal.Ignore.\n16\t// R1.6: safe to call multiple times; each invocation starts one goroutine.\n17\tfunc InstallSIGPIPEHandler() {\n18\t\tc := make(chan os.Signal, 1)\n19\t\tsignal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE)\n20\t\tgo func() {\n21\t\t\t<-c\n22\t\t\tos.Exit(0)\n23\t\t}()\n24\t}\n25\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018yFuWJ9nmYWnSxZktoG92j","session_id":"4a31301b-1723-4b6e-95a8-54479b7356ac","uuid":"2c13d1bd-95d5-4c39-81ef-7a6bc3d852c8","timestamp":"2026-05-14T12:43:30.190Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012JRGVHXvZdEAzV6SpS1JFG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n2\t// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n3\t\n4\t// Package sys provides portable syscall abstractions for terminal queries,\n5\t// extended file metadata, and signal handling on Darwin and Linux.\n6\t// Implements srd002-sys.\n7\tpackage sys\n8\t\n9\timport (\n10\t\t\"os\"\n11\t\t\"time\"\n12\t)\n13\t\n14\t// FileInfo holds extended file metadata from syscall.Stat_t that is not\n15\t// available through os.FileInfo alone.\n16\t// R2.2: struct fields match the package contract.\n17\ttype FileInfo struct {\n18\t\tMode       os.FileMode\n19\t\tSize       int64\n20\t\tNlink      uint64\n21\t\tUid        uint32\n22\t\tGid        uint32\n23\t\tRdev       uint64\n24\t\tDev        uint64\n25\t\tIno        uint64\n26\t\tBlocks     int64\n27\t\tBlksize    int64\n28\t\tModTime    time.Time\n29\t\tAccessTime time.Time\n30\t\tChangeTime time.Time\n31\t\tInfo       os.FileInfo\n32\t}\n33\t\n34\t// Stat returns extended file metadata for path, following symbolic links.\n35\t// R2.1: calls os.Stat and populates FileInfo from syscall.Stat_t.\n36\tfunc Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) {\n37\t\tinfo, err := os.Stat(path)\n38\t\tif err != nil {\n39\t\t\treturn nil, err\n40\t\t}\n41\t\treturn fileInfoFromOS(info)\n42\t}\n43\t\n44\t// Lstat returns extended file metadata for path without following symbolic links.\n45\t// R2.1: calls os.Lstat and populates FileInfo from syscall.Stat_t.\n46\tfunc Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) {\n47\t\tinfo, err := os.Lstat(path)\n48\t\tif err != nil {\n49\t\t\treturn nil, err\n50\t\t}\n51\t\treturn fileInfoFromOS(info)\n52\t}\n53\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018yFuWJ9nmYWnSxZktoG92j","session_id":"4a31301b-1723-4b6e-95a8-54479b7356ac","uuid":"6abf01cc-da91-4429-8263-22cf6a719c81","timestamp":"2026-05-14T12:43:30.190Z"}
